      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =\\frac{11}{36} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{121}{36} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-\\frac{11}{6}$ and $z=\\frac{11}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-\\frac{11}{6})$ and $(0,0,\\frac{11}{6}).$",
